{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789224230,"nonce":"kYimdqpFxGLl0pb0yN0wHAdNR7c=","title":"Promote module variables to locals across loops","message":"Top-level script loops keep their state in module variables (Wren has no locals at module scope), so every iteration loads and stores through the module cell and the values stay boxed because their types are unknown. bench/ash kernels at module level run at 7 ns per iteration versus under 1 ns in a method. A scalar-promotion pass over a loop region with no calls or module-variable escapes can load each module variable once before the loop, carry it as a block parameter, and store it back at every exit; with loop-carried type inference the arithmetic then unboxes. Store back before any call inside the loop, or restrict to call-free regions first.","files":null}]}